---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Peptide–MHC Binding Research"
  description="Explore Next Motion AI's first research focus: using adaptive search to study peptide–MHC binding."
>
  <main id="main-content">
    <section class="project-hero">
      <div class="container project-hero-grid">
        <div class="project-intro">
          <p class="eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow-line"></span> PROJECTS / 01</p>
          <h1>Starting with a question at the edge of <em>immunity.</em></h1>
          <p>Our first research focus is peptide–MHC binding: one early step in how the immune system encounters pieces of the biological world.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="project-figure">
          <img src="/img/peptide-mhc-concept.webp" alt="Conceptual illustration of a short peptide seated in an MHC protein binding groove" width="1536" height="1024" />
          <figcaption class="project-figure-label">ILLUSTRATIVE VIEW · NOT A RESEARCH RESULT</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="project-overview section-space" aria-labelledby="focus-heading">
      <div class="container project-overview-grid">
        <p class="eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow-line"></span> THE BIOLOGY</p>
        <div>
          <h2 id="focus-heading" class="display-heading">A small interaction with <em>wide implications.</em></h2>
          <div class="project-columns">
            <p>MHC proteins can bind short peptide fragments and display them for immune surveillance. Which peptides bind depends in part on the MHC variant. That makes binding a useful question to study, even though it is only one part of a larger biological process.</p>
            <p>We’re asking whether adaptive search can learn from binding feedback and prioritize promising peptides across a large candidate space. The goal is a better-supported set for follow-up evaluation, with biological claims reserved for appropriate experiments.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="project-status section-space" aria-labelledby="status-heading">
      <div class="container project-status-grid">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-light"><span class="eyebrow-line"></span> WHERE WE ARE</p>
          <h2 id="status-heading">Early work. <em>Open questions.</em></h2>
        </div>
        <div class="project-status-copy">
          <p>This is exploratory computational research. We are developing the tools and questions for the work; we are not reporting a discovered therapeutic candidate or a validated biological result.</p>
          <p>Binding alone does not establish that a peptide is presented on a cell, recognized by T cells, or useful as a medicine. Those questions call for other evidence.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="project-outro section-space">
      <div class="container project-outro-grid">
        <div><p class="eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow-line"></span> THE BROADER IDEA</p><h2 class="display-heading">Learn from each search. <em>Ask a better question.</em></h2></div>
        <div><p>This first focus is one place to explore how reinforcement learning might contribute to biological discovery. Our first research note explains the question and how we plan to evaluate it.</p><a class="text-link" href="/blog/2026-10-02-learning-from-feedback-in-peptide-discovery">Read the first research note <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
